" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-31907" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-31907" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-31907" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The system $Ax=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution\n- $A$ is invertible" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements II  and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-73563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-32088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-32088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-32088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-32088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-32088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-32088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-127943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-127943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements I and II are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-127943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-127943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-127943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-127943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement II is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- The system $Ax=b$ has infinitely many solutions\n- The lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k-8\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 8.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false.  According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-73570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-36075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=0$ has the unique  solution $⟨0,0⟩$\n- $⟨5,-4⟩$ is a solution to $Ax=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-36075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-36075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-36075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-36075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-36075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x+2y=1 \\\\ -3x+ky=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true: \n\nIf the system's coefficient matrix is invertible then $\\text{__________}\\,.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements. \n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible then $det(A)\\ne 0.$    \n\nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the two lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2.$ \n\nFinally, notice that if $k=-1$ then \n\n$$\ndet[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=6\\cdot (-1)-2(-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, if $A$ is invertible,  then $k\\ne -1.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the system's coefficient matrix is invertible, then the lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-73596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]=0"] ["math" "k=-1"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  has infinite solutions"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$  has no solution"] ["text" "The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-73596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution\n- $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$* $\\,✓$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-128139" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128139" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128139" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-31921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the lines $2x+y=2$ and $x+ky=5$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-31921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $2x+y=2$ and $x+ky=5$ intersect at one point, then\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is invertible.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-31921" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-31921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-31921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is solution to $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]x=0$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is invertible"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-31921" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is invertible"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has \n no solution"] ["text" "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is the solution to $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-128154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-47657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the unique solution of $Ax=0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does not exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-47657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\,✓$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-47657" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5451afbc5e7a8376cec33ffd7224c0a1de532ca93c8a79fd1541bdb67ac9714f"} {"question" "q-47657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-47657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "q-47657" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/state.json" "file_sha256" "2902cfa8af8d6817f39200ba1bf0c76b9405a7e73830bd532f96cbfdf369de9a"} {"question" "e-4511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) =0$\n\n- The system ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/0b4ad54b702a4c7dc8cca849c32d035e558a8b91d8df22b14eb65b28f41093b0.png) has exactly one solution\n\n- $A^{-1}$ does not exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4511.json" "file_sha256" "c1bb402f56bac7ac9640064e219ffc5e6e337ed64a0fbfa16233ae6202f78666"} {"question" "e-4511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/79f95782298c108b174ce190af7b3a073fa0f704072932967c4f16313c955ce7.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4511.json" "file_sha256" "c1bb402f56bac7ac9640064e219ffc5e6e337ed64a0fbfa16233ae6202f78666"} {"question" "e-4512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/15ba3333a2ced8790efd81a35d26be43d2f0841b9529bf9d6b5b5e1c0e9f9b67.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/18978e96f80b0ba3d0f997c1db6162886b9d9e2d2c5ee7d5925a48b6147081af.png) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4512.json" "file_sha256" "f45bcc8e24bd051d3499e97e126072ef50853bc401fb19936a0faea36d8ccd0a"} {"question" "e-4512" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/e49dcc022775cc8eb5317611a3fae4d21892a7c566989f67f65e5213ee5c6b9d.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/example-4512.json" "file_sha256" "f45bcc8e24bd051d3499e97e126072ef50853bc401fb19936a0faea36d8ccd0a"} {"question" "e-4513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\