ents are true?\n\n1. $A$ is invertible\n2. $det(A) \\ne 0$\n3. $Ax = 0$ has an infinite number of solutions"] [17592186115662 :question/problem "Let\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $det(A) = 0$\n2. $Ax = b$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\cdot 1$ column vector $b$\n3. $A^{-1}$ does **not** exist"] [17592186115653 :question/problem "Consider the lines $2x + y = 2$ and $x + ky = 5$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n> *If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\mid __________$.*"] [17592186115653 :question/answer-fields 17592186113318] [17592186114982 :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\det(A) =0$\n2. The system $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution\n3. $A^{-1}$ does not exist"] [17592186114982 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [17592186114983 :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\det(A) = 0$\n2. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n3. The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point"] [17592186114983 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n$det(A)|= det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot k - (-2) \\cdot (-2); = k - 4$,\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4$. So, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$.\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] [17592186114984 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n> *If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$.*\n\n1. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n2. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n3. $\\det(A) = 0$"] [17592186114984 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186113319 17592186113320 17592186113321 17592186113322 17592186113323 17592186113373 17592186113374 17592186113375 17592186113376 17592186113377 17592186113704 17592186113705 17592186113706 17592186113707 17592186113708] "immutable_fields" [[17592186113318 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113318 :answer-field/id] [17592186113318 :answer-field/key] [17592186113318 :answer-field/type] [17592186113372 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113372 :answer-field/id] [17592186113372 :answer-field/key] [17592186113372 :answer-field/type] [17592186113703 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113703 :answer-field/id] [17592186113703 :answer-field/key] [17592186113703 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
